    "On a cold Thursday evening, after a rehearsal* in which she had played the wrong notes three times, Wren sat on her bed and opened the case. {1|She meant to clean the violin one last time before returning it.} Under the soft cloth at the bottom, her fingers touched something stiff. It was an envelope, yellow with age, tucked behind a loose corner of the lining*.",
    "Inside was a single sheet of paper. The handwriting was round and neat, the writing of a young person trying hard to be tidy. \"To whoever plays this violin next,\" it began. \"Today I nearly quit. I told my teacher I was the worst player in the orchestra, and maybe that was true. {2|She said that nobody sounds good at the beginning, and that the only way to get through the difficult part is to keep going until it stops being difficult.} I don't know if she is right. But I have decided to try for one more year. If you are reading this on a bad day, maybe you can try too.\"",
    "There was no name at the bottom, only a date. The letter had been written thirty-one years earlier.",
